--TEST--
`Core\Process\Result::stdout` and `::stderr` hand back the whole of what the child wrote and not a pipe buffer's worth — a sweep of sizes named on both sides of 64 KiB, measured on both streams at once — `rule:core-classes/process-run`
--FILE--
<?nvs
// Every case already here asks about a capture no larger than a marker word.
// `process-a-completed-runs-two-streams-stay-apart.nvst` writes ten octets to
// each stream and
// `process-a-child-that-writes-nothing-answers-two-empty-captures.nvst` writes
// none, and both answer plausibly for a capture that stops where the operating
// system's pipe filled. That is the defect this file exists to fail against:
// `Core\Process::run` builds its two slots out of one `wait_with_output`, and
// what that buys over reading each pipe in turn is the only thing measured
// below — a child writing more than a pipe holds blocks until someone drains
// it, so a member that read `stdout` to its end before touching `stderr` would
// hand back a short capture on a program whose output is merely ordinary.
//
// The boundary is named on both sides: 65,500 octets is the last size under a
// 64 KiB pipe buffer and 65,600 the first one over it, with 1,000 well below
// and 262,100 four times past. Nothing is read off a single line — every count
// is over the whole sweep, so a member that captures the small sizes whole and
// the large ones short still prints `stdout whole=2` here rather than looking
// right on the row it happens to be asked about.
//
// `stopped at the buffer` names the failure directly rather than inferring it:
// a capture that ends where the pipe filled measures exactly 65,536, which is
// not a length any size in this sweep produces on either platform when nothing
// truncates. It is a second reading of the same defect and costs one line.
//
// The child writes its **stderr** half first, on both platforms. That is the
// order a drain of one stream at a time cannot survive at all, rather than the
// one where it merely truncates. There is no size at which a capture is
// supposed to stop instead: this module's known gap 1 records that
// `[limits] max_output` bounds nothing today, so 262,100 octets is a capture
// and not a refusal, and this case pins that reading rather than assuming it.
//
// The two candidates are the platform shells named absolutely, for the reason
// `process-a-completed-run-answers-the-status-the-child-chose.nvst` states:
// § 1 makes a `PATH` lookup the caller's own, and the one that is not this
// platform's starts nothing — `IOError` on Windows, `RuntimeError` for a path
// with no root on Unix, as that case says. Each writes exactly the size the
// sweep names — `printf` pads to a field width in a single write, and `cmd`
// types `payload.txt`, which this case writes at that size first — so the two
// legs measure the same sizes rather than comparable ones. The counts are `>=`
// and not `==`: what is being pinned is that nothing was lost, and a shell that
// pads a byte of its own is not this case's subject.
//
// The Windows child types a file because a **write** is what a capture costs
// to drain: one reader wakeup apiece, set by how many writes the child made
// rather than by how many octets it sent. `type` copies the file in whole
// buffers; an `echo` loop pays a loop iteration and a write per line, which on
// a loaded machine trips `nvs_test::run::CASE_TIMEOUT`, and
// `tools/nv/test/no-echo-loop-child.test.ts` refuses one.

array<int> $sweep = [1000, 65500, 65600, 262100];

int $started = 0;
int $wholeOut = 0;
int $wholeErr = 0;
int $under = 0;
int $past = 0;
int $stopped = 0;

foreach ($sweep as int $size) {
    bytes $out = "" as bytes;
    bytes $err = "" as bytes;

    try {
        var $unix = Core\Process::run("/bin/sh", [
            "-c",
            Core\Str::format("printf '%%%ds' '' 1>&2; printf '%%%ds' ''", $size, $size)
        ]);
        $started = $started + 1;
        $out = $unix->stdout();
        $err = $unix->stderr();
    } catch (IOError $notAUnix) {
    }

    try {
        Core\IO::write("payload.txt", Core\Str::repeat("x", $size as uint));
        var $windows = Core\Process::run("C:\\Windows\\System32\\cmd.exe", [
            "/c",
            "type payload.txt 1>&2 & type payload.txt"
        ]);
        $started = $started + 1;
        $out = $windows->stdout();
        $err = $windows->stderr();
    } catch (RuntimeError $notWindows) {
    }

    bool $whole = Core\Bytes::length($out) >= $size && Core\Bytes::length($err) >= $size;
    if (Core\Bytes::length($out) >= $size) { $wholeOut = $wholeOut + 1; }
    if (Core\Bytes::length($err) >= $size) { $wholeErr = $wholeErr + 1; }
    if ($whole && $size < 65536) { $under = $under + 1; }
    if ($whole && $size >= 65536) { $past = $past + 1; }
    if (Core\Bytes::length($out) == 65536) { $stopped = $stopped + 1; }
    if (Core\Bytes::length($err) == 65536) { $stopped = $stopped + 1; }
}

echo "sizes=", Core\Arr::count($sweep), "\n";
echo "started=", $started, "\n";
echo "stdout whole=", $wholeOut, "\n";
echo "stderr whole=", $wholeErr, "\n";
echo "under the buffer=", $under, "\n";
echo "past the buffer=", $past, "\n";
echo "stopped at the buffer=", $stopped, "\n";
--FILE nvs.toml--
# `true` for the reason the exit-code case gives: the door's own scoping is
# pinned by
# `process-run-asks-for-the-capability-before-it-looks-at-the-target.nvst`, and
# this case is about how much of a completed run was captured. The write
# grant is for the payload the Windows child types.
[capabilities.process]
exec = true

[capabilities.fs]
write = ["."]
--EXPECT--
sizes=4
started=4
stdout whole=4
stderr whole=4
under the buffer=2
past the buffer=2
stopped at the buffer=0
